## .NET references through msil-ld (ImplementationPlan.md 5.21.10 RL1-RL7,
## RV27 of 5.21.14; milestone R-a). A reference is a C value of type 'T ^'
## for a CLI_TYPE struct T (5.21.1 RA1): target("msil.ref") in IR (5.21.6
## RI1) and 'object' in IL (RI3). A managed call passes it with the
## conversion codes 'ref' and 'this-ref' (5.21.9 RM1, RM2, RM7); a C cast to
## another class calls a cast stub '__msil_mc.<castclass>.__uniq.<n>', whose
## body is 'ldarg.0; castclass <TYPE>; ret' (RM8); and '==' calls the helper
## '__msil_ref.eq' (RI6), which instruction selection turns into ceq, beq,
## bne.un, brtrue or brfalse (5.21.7 RB5), so that no object names it. The
## members are Inputs/fakepack/FakeWidgets.il's, over the core library
## FakeRuntime.il (managed-calls.test). Each input is IR in the form that
## clang -O2 -flto writes for the C in its comments (without target
## attributes, debug information and summary, and with its values renamed),
## or hand-written IR where it says so.
## - Binding with references (RL1, RL3, RL5; 5.20.7 MK1): calls.ll on
##   x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu-msil and
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet. Each object's link-info lists every stub, the
##   cast stubs among them, and the static functions pick and use_casts as
##   LOCAL FUNCTION symbols, and no '__msil_ref' symbol (SYMHDR, SYMTAB).
##   Each object links with --reference without a diagnostic. In the image
##   every member access, and each newarr and isinst token, names its type
##   in FakeWidgets or FakeRuntime (BIND); each cast stub is exactly
##   'ldarg.0; castclass <TYPE>; ret', a stub with no member access, for a
##   class, System.String, int32[], string[], class Contoso.Widget[], the
##   nested class Contoso.Outer/Inner and the interface Contoso.IWidget
##   (CASTS); a 'ref' argument is 'ldarg' and a 'castclass' to the member's
##   parameter type, none for 'object', a 'ref' result is returned as it
##   is, and 'this-ref' is 'ldarg.0', a 'castclass' to TYPE (none for
##   System.Object) and then 'callvirt', for the method that is not virtual
##   (Resize) too, or 'ldfld' (RM7); a field of a class type gives a 'ref'
##   result: the instance field Part, a Contoso.Outer/Inner, is 'ldarg.0',
##   a 'castclass' to Contoso.Widget, which declares it, and 'ldfld', and
##   the static field Last, a Contoso.Widget, is 'ldsfld', each returned as
##   it is, and their MemberRefs name both classes in FakeWidgets (BIND);
##   with msil_catch the result local is 'object' and the handler's zero is
##   'ldnull' (RM7, RM12); the signatures of the stubs and of the static
##   functions and the locals of main and use_casts hold 'object' (RL5)
##   (STUBS). The images refer to FakeRuntime and FakeWidgets only, and no
##   line names '__msil_ref' (REFS).
## - Without references (MK1): table.ll's managed calls of built-in table
##   members (GetCommandLineArgs() and BitConverter::GetBytes(int32) with
##   'ref' results, StringToHGlobalUni(string) with a String ^, the
##   EntryPointNotFoundException constructor, Exception::get_HResult() and
##   Encoding::GetString(uint8*, int32) through 'this-ref', UTF8Encoding's
##   constructor, String::.ctor(char*), and GCHandle's Alloc and get_Target
##   with tickets) and its casts to System.Exception, System.String and
##   string[] and, from a UTF8Encoding ^, to System.Text.Encoding link on each
##   triple without a diagnostic and refer to [mscorlib] only (TABLE and
##   TCAST with S=[mscorlib], TREFS-LIB); with --reference the Windows and
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet objects bind to FakeRuntime instead
##   (S=[FakeRuntime], TREFS-FAKE).
## - LTO and text: calls.ll as bitcode links in full LTO on a host triple
##   and on msil64-unknown-dotnet, and as one ThinLTO module, which imports
##   nothing, so pick and use_casts stay internal, with the same cast stubs.
##   Under --lto-emit-asm it gives the same text without and with
##   --reference (with references msil-ld checks every declaration, RL1 and
##   RL2, and finds no error), whose stubs print 5.20.6 MW2's kind check
##   ('castclass class [mscorlib]T' for a class, interface or array of a
##   class; System.String keeps its TypeRef), and whose field stubs give
##   the class of Part and of Last as 'class [mscorlib]T' too (TEXT), which
##   names no '__msil_ref' and imports nothing (TXTHDR), and which ilasm
##   assembles.
##   Under --lto-partitions=2 the partition split makes the static functions
##   external and hidden, and slot P1 refuses each function with a
##   reference in its signature that remains, the definition and a
##   declaration in the other partition alike (RL7; 5.21.7 RB11 rule 3,
##   T-RB2, sorted):
##   'a function with a .NET reference in its signature that ThinLTO or --lto-partitions made external (link with full LTO and --lto-partitions=1) is not supported for MSIL yet',
##   after the '<unknown>:0:0: in function <f> <type>: ' that msil-ld prints
##   for a back-end error of an LTO link.
## - T-M16 (RL2, RL3; 5.20.7 MK3): kinds.ll names the struct Contoso.Point
##   as a class (CLI_TYPE("Contoso.Point")). With references its 'this-ref'
##   TYPE (point_sum), its 'ref' parameter (wrap_point, whose member the
##   binder then does not find, 5.11.9) and its cast stub, named
##   '<castclass>', are each
##   'managed call '<f>': 'Contoso.Point' is a value type, but the declaration uses it as a reference type'
##   (sorted): from the objects' castclass tokens on a host triple and on
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet, in an LTO link, and from the declarations under
##   --lto-emit-asm. A class (Contoso.Widget), an interface (Contoso.IWidget)
##   and an array (int32[]) get no error. Without references --lto-emit-asm
##   links and its text makes the kind check when the program runs (KTEXT),
##   and the object fails with 5.11.7's text for each type outside the table
##   (sorted).
## - Declarations that slot P0 refuses (RL1; bad.ll): msil-ld takes 'ref',
##   'this-ref' and RM8's cast shape as P0 does, so it skips 'ref' for an
##   int32 parameter or with a pointer in IR, 'this-ref' of a value type or
##   after the first parameter, a cast stub of System.Object and one with
##   "msil-managed-decl", and each gets P0's error once, in its order, in an
##   image link and under --lto-emit-asm, with and without references:
##   'managed call '<f>' has invalid "msil-managed-*" attributes: <reason>'.
## - The helper is never bound (RL4, RL6): helper.ll compares two
##   references from managed calls, so its call of '__msil_ref.eq' survives
##   LTO's optimizer. It links without a diagnostic in full LTO at -O2 and
##   -O0, under --lto-partitions=2, as a ThinLTO module, on
##   msil64-unknown-dotnet, under --lto-emit-asm without and with
##   --reference and at -O0, and beside a native library that exports
##   '__msil_ref.eq' (helper.lib on Windows, libhelper.so on Linux), in
##   images and in text: main compares with 'ceq', and no output names the
##   helper, imports it or names a native library (HELPER, HTEXT).
##   hidden.ll declares the helper and the managed call hidden (the managed
##   call as clang declares it under '#pragma GCC visibility push(hidden)'),
##   and links too, in an image and under --lto-emit-asm without and with
##   --reference: msil-ld neither binds nor reports an undefined
##   '__msil_ref.' or '__msil_mc.' function, hidden or not, before LTO's
##   code generation, which defines the stub and lowers the helper's calls
##   (5.20.7 MK5). Another '__msil_ref.' name (other.ll's
##   '__msil_ref.ne') is not bound or reported by msil-ld: code generation
##   refuses it (5.21.7 RB11 rule 11, T-RB7):
##   ''<name>' is reserved for the MSIL toolchain'.
## - T-RL1 (RL4): a definition of a '__msil_ref.' name is
##   '<file>: defines '<name>', which is reserved for the MSIL toolchain (a reference helper)'
##   for each GLOBAL, WEAK or COMMON function or variable: in bitcode
##   (def.ll, in its symbol table's order, functions first) in full LTO,
##   under --lto-emit-asm, under --lto-partitions=2 and as a ThinLTO module,
##   on a host triple and on msil64-unknown-dotnet; in a CIL object, in its
##   symbol order (variables first): defobj.ll's, which llc compiles under
##   '__msil_REF.' names and rename.py renames, as an object of another
##   toolchain might define them (slot P1 refuses a '__msil_ref.' function
##   definition, T-RB7); and in an archive member that a bitcode reference
##   to the helper extracts, 'libdef.a(defobj.o)'. Internal definitions get
##   no error. An object never references the helper (RB5), so the member
##   is never extracted for one.
## - ThinLTO (RL7): thin-m.ll's main calls thin-a.ll's g, which calls the
##   static size_of, whose parameter is a reference. Full LTO links. As
##   ThinLTO modules, in either input order, main's module imports g, so
##   size_of is promoted, external and hidden, as 'size_of.llvm.<n>': its
##   definition and the declaration that main's module calls once it has
##   inlined g each get T-RB2. Under --lto-partitions=2 the definition and
##   the declaration in the other partition each get T-RB2 too.
## - T-RB5 through the LTO table pass (5.21.7 RB12): under the module flag
##   msil-lto, MSILFixFunctionBitcasts adapts a call whose type differs from
##   the definition's by 5.9.8's table, before LTO's optimizer
##   (obj-lto.test). A difference that involves a reference is
##   'call of '<g>' passes '<A>' where it takes '<P>': a .NET reference cannot be converted'
##   and never an adapter: take-ref.ll passes a reference where take.ll's
##   take takes a pointer, passes an extra reference to count (the table
##   would drop an extra argument with a warning) and expects a reference
##   from get, which returns a pointer; hold-ptr.ll passes a pointer to
##   hold.ll's reference parameter. In full LTO at -O2 and -O0, under
##   --lto-emit-asm and on msil64-unknown-dotnet, in either input order.
## - Cast stubs and LTO (5.21.9 RM8): cast-a.ll and cast-b.ll each cast to
##   Contoso.Widget. Linked as objects, each object keeps its own LOCAL stub
##   (the second renamed '<name><msil>1', as obj-resolve.test's locals); in
##   full LTO the two declarations, equal in name and operands, are one stub,
##   with no T-M17 (TWO, ONE). A -shared library exports one() and neither
##   the stub nor the managed calls.

# REQUIRES: llvm-cil-dump
# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
# RUN: mkdir refs none withrefs helper merge
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeRuntime.yaml -o refs/FakeRuntime.dll
# RUN: yaml2obj %S/Inputs/fakepack/FakeWidgets.yaml -o refs/FakeWidgets.dll
# DEFINE: %{crash} = --implicit-check-not="PLEASE submit" --implicit-check-not="Stack dump" \
# DEFINE:   --implicit-check-not="LLVM ERROR"
# DEFINE: %{err} = FileCheck %s %{crash} --implicit-check-not=error: --implicit-check-not=warning:
## An image that refers to FakeRuntime and FakeWidgets only and names no
## '__msil_ref' anywhere: REFS has only the assembly references, so the
## implicit check-nots cover every other line.
# DEFINE: %{refs} = FileCheck %s --check-prefix=REFS --implicit-check-not=assemblyref --implicit-check-not=mscorlib \
# DEFINE:   --implicit-check-not=msil.bcl --implicit-check-not=__msil_ref

## Binding with references, through objects, on each triple.
# RUN: llc -filetype=obj -mtriple=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil calls.ll -o win.o
# RUN: llvm-cil-dump --link-info win.o > win.sym
# RUN: FileCheck %s %{crash} --check-prefix=SYMHDR --input-file=win.sym -DTRIPLE=x86_64-pc-windows-msvc-msil \
# RUN:   --implicit-check-not=__msil_ref --implicit-check-not="{{(GLOBAL|WEAK) .*__msil_mc}}"
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=SYMTAB --input-file=win.sym
# RUN: msil-ld win.o --reference=refs -o win.exe 2>&1 | count 0
# RUN: llvm-cil-dump win.exe > win.txt
# RUN: FileCheck %s --check-prefix=BIND --input-file=win.txt
